is a decision rule, not a point forecast."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-dd4a511267f1428ca087dbab15729907", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-dd4a511267f1428ca087dbab15729907", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"stage": "result", "summary": "Submit complete experiment design deliverable with fixed scaffold, statistics, feasibility, and ledger", "answer": "# Experiment design: Does an OPRO-optimized instruction beat \"Let's think step by step.\" and the empty prompt on BBH?\n\nSTATUS: Design-only deliverable. No experiment was executed; nothing here is an observed result. The workspace (verified below) contains no runnable assets, so this document is a self-contained specification that would be executed in an environment with LLM access and the public BBH release. Items that are proposals or defaults are explicitly labeled [PROPOSAL] or [DEFAULT]; items marked [NEEDS VERIFICATION] must be confirmed against the cited sources before execution; nothing is asserted as an established fact unless so labeled.\n\nREPRODUCTION TARGET: The design mirrors, with deviations made explicit, the instruction-optimization methodology of Yang et al. (2023), \"Large Language Models as Optimizers\" (arXiv:2309.03409, \"OPRO\"), applied to BIG-Bench Hard (BBH; Suzgun et al. 2022/2023).\n\nRESEARCH QUESTION: For each BBH subtask, does the top instruction discovered by the OPRO loop (scored on a dev split) yield higher test accuracy than (i) \"Let's think step by step.\" and (ii) an empty instruction, given an otherwise identical prompt, model, decoding, and data?\n\nTARGET QUANTITIES:\n- Primary: per-task exact-match test accuracy, acc_c(t), for condition c \u2208 {opt, ltsbs, empty}, task t \u2208 {1..23}.\n- Primary comparison: per-task paired delta \u0394_c(t) = acc_opt(t) \u2212 acc_c(t) (c = ltsbs, empty).\n- Secondary: macro-average over the 23 tasks; wins/losses/ties; per-task descriptive tests (FDR-corrected).\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n## (a) RATIONALE\n\n1. ALL 23 BBH subtasks, not a subset. BBH is defined as a hard multi-task suite; its value is per-task characterization. Subsetting invites cherry-picking and forfeits the across-task paired test that is our primary inference device [PROPOSAL, but mandated in scope]. No task is excluded a priori.\n\n2. OPRO-as-optimizer. \"Optimized\" is given the OPRO meaning: a meta-prompt drives an optimizer LLM to propose instruction candidates; each candidate is scored by the task LLM on the dev split; a trajectory of the best-scoring instructions (with their dev accuracy) is appended to the meta-prompt for the next round. The final instruction is the best dev-scored candidate. This is the construct that the question targets [DEFAULT, mirrors arXiv:2309.03409; exact recipe verified below].\n\n3. Baselines as instruction-string controls. \"Let's think step by step.\" is the canonical human CoT instruction; empty is the \"no instruction\" control. The scaffold, exemplars, model, and decoding are identical across all three conditions; the only inter-condition difference is the instruction string. This isolates the instruction as the causal variable.\n\n4. One model for both optimizer and evaluator. OPRO used a single model family (PaLM 2) in both roles; reusing one model keeps the optimization signal aligned with the evaluation and removes a cross-model confound. Named model = PaLM 2-L for faithful reproduction; if unavailable, a documented open model (e.g., Llama-3-70B-Instruct) with the SAME model in both roles [ASSUMPTION \u2014 conditional conclusion, stated in every report].\n\n5. Paired, across-task inference. Within a task, the same test examples, model, and decoding are measured under two instruction strings, so \u0394(t) is a paired difference; the 23 tasks are independent units \u2192 paired statistics over 23 deltas. Per-task n \u2248 250 is underpowered for per-task significance (see analysis plan), so the primary claim is at the across-task level and per-task tests are descriptive only.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n## (b) CONTROLS\n\nThe following are held FIXED and identical across all three conditions and all 23 tasks (deviation log must record any exception):\n\nC1. Model: same single LLM, same frozen checkpoint/version, same inference parameters for all conditions (optimizer and task roles may share inference config where sensible) [DEFAULT].\n\nC2. Prompt scaffold (concrete, fixed). Only the <INSTR> slot varies between conditions. Template per example:\n```\n<INSTR>\n\n{exemplar_1}\n{exemplar_2}\n{exemplar_3}\n\nQ: {question}\nA:\n```\nThe three exemplars are the standard BBH few-shot exemplars for the task, drawn from the official BBH release and HELD IDENTICAL across conditions; they provide the answer-format elicitation (each exemplar ends with \"\u2026 so the answer is {gold}.\"). They are excluded from both the dev scoring set and the test set. <INSTR> is set to: the final optimized instruction (opt); \"Let's think step by step.\" (ltsbs); empty string (empty). This scaffold is the FIXED DEFINITION of the empty-prompt condition: exemplars + question + answer elicitation, blank instruction slot [DEFAULT; the exact template wording is a proposal that must be identical across conditions].\n\nC3. Data splits, per task: a fixed DEV split (default 100 examples from the BBH train split, disjoint from the 3 exemplars) used ONLY for optimization scoring [DEFAULT, size needs verification vs paper]; a fixed TEST split (BBH test split, ~250 examples [NEEDS VERIFICATION on exact counts]) used ONLY for the final per-condition evaluation, each condition evaluated exactly once. No test example is ever seen during optimization, under any condition.\n\nC4. Decoding: greedy (T=0) for BOTH dev scoring and final evaluation [DEFAULT; paper's decoding at test time needs verification]. Fixed max_tokens and stop conditions, set so that answer extraction is feasible. If the paper is found to use sampled decoding, its temperature/samples become the fixed config for all conditions.\n\nC5. Answer extraction/metric: exact-match accuracy against BBH gold, after canonical normalization (BBH gold answers are short values \u2014 letters, True/False, numbers, names; normalize whitespace/case; use the official BBH answer-parse rules if available [NEEDS VERIFICATION on the exact normalization]). Last-line \"the answer is X\" pattern is parsed; a missing/unparseable answer counts as incorrect for ALL conditions (this may penalize the empty condition; that is accepted and reported).\n\nC6. Optimization budget: identical for all tasks and all conditions/scenarios \u2014 a fixed number of OPRO steps (default 200) and candidates/step (default 8) [DEFAULT; both need verification vs paper]. Stopping is by budget only, never by early-achieved dev accuracy (prevents per-task budget differences from confounding).\n\nC7. Repetitions: R = 3 independent optimization runs per task, differing only in the random seed of the optimizer's decoding. Same dev/test splits for all runs and all conditions. Final test accuracy per task per condition = mean over the 3 runs' instructions evaluated on the same test split [DEFAULT, locked by scope].\n\nC8. Reproducibility artifacts: commit the exact template, exemplars, splits, seeds, model version, all inference parameters, the full meta-prompt recipe, and a log of every instruction candidate and dev score (audit trail). Any OPRO-paper 